# eu-u9xj.6 (PP) fixture for the FORK half of
# test_pp_gc_collects_during_par_map in harness_test.rs.
#
# The driver has to hold heap handles across `force()` calls: mapping N
# elements means accumulating N results while forcing each one. A handle on
# the Rust stack is invisible to the collector, so if the driver kept them
# there, a collection during the map would move the objects out from under it
# and leave it dereferencing freed or relocated memory. They live in the
# machine's root set instead (`IntrinsicMachine::gc_root_push`).
#
# `EU_GC_VERIFY=2` cannot see that class of bug — it verifies reachability
# *from roots*, and the whole point is that these handles were not roots. What
# catches it is running the map with collection actually happening during it,
# under `EU_GC_STRESS=1` so every collection evacuates rather than merely
# marking, and comparing the answer with the `map` oracle beside this file.
#
# Four times the work of pp_gc_stress_fixture.eu, because the fork path
# splits it W ways: each worker must allocate enough *within its own chunk* to
# reach the limit, or no collection happens in the worker and the gate does
# not gate. Verified by fault injection at W=4 with --heap-limit-mib 8 —
# removing the root set from the worker loop is detected there and is NOT
# detected on the smaller shape.
#
# The shape is tuned so a collection lands mid-map: the heap limit the test
# passes is crossed partway through (this heap collects only once its limit is
# reached), and each element allocates a 60-element intermediate list. Raise
# the limit enough and the run finishes without collecting at all and the gate
# stops gating — at `--heap-limit-mib 64` this same shape completes in 0.1s and
# detects nothing. It is deliberately small: once the limit is reached every
# poll collects, so cost grows steeply with the work done afterwards. If this
# fixture is ever retuned, re-verify by fault injection that it still fails
# when the root set is removed.

f(x): range(0, 100) map((_0 + x)) sum
xs: range(1, 60)
r: xs par-map(f)
n: r sum
